    "model": "Method 1: interval training.\nInterval training involves repeated short maximal efforts, such as 10 sprints of 40 metres with 45 seconds of walking recovery. This matches the stop-start demands of hurling, building the anaerobic energy systems needed for sprints and the aerobic capacity to clear by-products between efforts, so the player can keep up the pace in the last quarter.\nMethod 2: weight training.\nWeight training uses resistance to build muscular strength and power, for example heavy squats and power cleans performed for three to five sets. Stronger legs and core let the player win the ball in contact and strike harder, and the loading strengthens tendons and ligaments to reduce injury risk.",
