    "model": "Method: I compared men's and women's elite Gaelic games coverage in newspapers, TV and social media over one season, counting articles and broadcast minutes.\nVolume: Women's sport got only a small share of coverage, often under ten percent, while men's fixtures were reported routinely.\nQuality: Women were more often described by appearance or family role, whereas men were described by skill and strength.\nPlacement: Women's results appeared on back pages and in short slots, while men's games got headlines and prime-time broadcasts.\nEffect: Low coverage means fewer sponsors, fewer role models and lower participation by girls.\nChange: Coverage rises around major finals and when broadcasters invest, and social media now lets governing bodies reach fans directly.",
